{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"ecosystem/ui-design","track":"everyone","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"UI Design","words":1016,"objectives":{"lang":"en","items":["Explain why AI-made screens look like they came from different companies","Say what a design system is and how DESIGN.md gives it to the AI","Name what generated screens still get wrong, especially accessibility"]},"translatedObjectives":null,"prerequisites":[],"previous":{"key":"ecosystem/inference-engines","number":37,"title":"Inference Engines","drawn":true,"track":"everyone","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"ecosystem/choosing-tech-stack","number":39,"title":"Choosing a Tech Stack","drawn":false,"track":"engineers","lang":"en"},"toc":[{"id":"why-this-matters-to-you","text":"Why This Matters to You","depth":2},{"id":"two-tools-that-turn-a-description-into-a-screen","text":"Two Tools That Turn a Description into a Screen","depth":2},{"id":"a-design-system-and-why-the-agent-needs-it-in-a-file","text":"A Design System, and Why the Agent Needs it in a File","depth":2},{"id":"designmd","text":"DESIGN.md","depth":2},{"id":"what-they-still-do-not-do","text":"What They Still Do Not Do","depth":2},{"id":"words-you-now-know","text":"Words You Now Know","depth":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","depth":2},{"id":"references","text":"References","depth":2},{"id":"-articles","text":"📄 Articles","depth":3},{"id":"️-tools","text":"🛠️ Tools","depth":3},{"id":"️-repositories","text":"🗃️ Repositories","depth":3}],"body":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"An AI that writes code will happily build you a screen. The problem is that it builds a different one every time. Ask for a settings page today and a dashboard tomorrow, and you get two screens that look like they came from two different companies: different colours, different button shapes, different headings. Each looks fine alone. Together, it looks like nobody was in charge."}],"lead":true},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This module is about the tools that design screens, and the simple fix for that problem."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-this-matters-to-you","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why This Matters to You"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"If you build a website or an internal tool by describing it, as this course promises you can, you will meet this problem on your second screen. The fix is one file, and it is the same idea as the onboarding note from "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"intermediate/coding-agents","track":"everyone","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Code Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"two-tools-that-turn-a-description-into-a-screen","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Two Tools That Turn a Description into a Screen"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://stitch.withgoogle.com/"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/ecosystem/logos/google.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"Google Stitch"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" takes a description, an image, a rough sketch or a spoken description, and produces a finished design with real code behind it. It can also send the design to Figma, the tool many designers use."}]},{"type":"image","number":"38.1","title":"Google Stitch","note":"Stitch's own overview. You describe a design in words, by voice (\"Listening...\") or with an image or a link, then preview and edit it. Notice the \"Design.md\" tab in its design system panel: it is the file the DESIGN.md section below is about.","src":"course-images/ecosystem/google-stitch-ui.webp","alt":"Google Stitch","width":1725,"height":1131},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://claude.ai/design"},"inlines":[{"type":"logo","src":"course-images/ecosystem/logos/claude.svg","alt":"","mono":false},{"type":"text","text":"Claude Design"}]}]},{"type":"text","text":" from Anthropic, launched in April 2026, makes prototypes, slides and one-page documents as real, working code. It can also read an existing project, find the design rules already in it, and follow them for whatever you ask next."}]},{"type":"image","number":"38.2","title":"Claude Design's templates","note":"Claude Design starts from one request and a template: a mobile app, slides, a document, a wireframe and more. The \"Design system\" menu picks a set of design decisions before anything is drawn.","src":"course-images/ecosystem/claude-design-types.webp","alt":"Claude Design's templates","width":1043,"height":664},{"type":"image","number":"38.3","title":"Tweaking a prototype in Claude Design","note":"A prototype being adjusted in Claude Design. The Tweaks panel changes the device, dark mode, the colour theme and the text sizes without a new prompt.","src":"course-images/ecosystem/claude-design-ui.webp","alt":"Tweaking a prototype in Claude Design","width":1280,"height":720},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Between the two, the pattern is clear: describing a screen is now faster than drawing one, and the result is working code, not a picture."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"a-design-system-and-why-the-agent-needs-it-in-a-file","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A Design System, and Why the Agent Needs it in a File"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"design system"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the set of design decisions a brand makes once, so nobody has to make them again: the colours and what each is for, the fonts and sizes, the spacing, the corner shapes, and how a button looks when you hover over it or press it. If you have seen a company's brand guidelines from marketing, that is the same idea."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"An AI building a screen has to make every one of those decisions. It cannot know yours, so it invents ones that look reasonable, and different ones each time. The fix is the same as for instructions: write the decisions down in a file, and let the AI read it."}]},{"type":"image","number":"38.4","title":"A design system next to a screen built from it","note":"A design system in Google Stitch (left): its colours with their exact codes, three text styles, buttons and icons. On the right is a fitness dashboard generated from it. Every next screen you ask for, such as the suggested \"workout details screen\" below it, reuses the same decisions.","src":"course-images/ecosystem/example-design-system.webp","alt":"A design system next to a screen built from it","width":1300,"height":733},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"designmd","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"DESIGN.md"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"code","text":"DESIGN.md"},{"type":"text","text":" is that file. It sits in the project folder, and most AI tools read it automatically. It needs two kinds of content:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The exact values"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the colour codes, font sizes, spacing steps and corner shapes."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The intent, in words"}]},{"type":"text","text":": what the design should feel like, what it avoids on purpose, and why. A list of values cannot say this part."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"The demonstration is very direct. Give an AI one request with a "},{"type":"code","text":"DESIGN.md"},{"type":"text","text":" modelled on Stripe's style, and you get Stripe's colours and spacing. Swap in another brand's file and change nothing else, and the result matches the new brand."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Notice the three files this course has now met. "},{"type":"code","text":"AGENTS.md"},{"type":"text","text":" holds the rules for working in a project. "},{"type":"code","text":"SOUL.md"},{"type":"text","text":", from "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"intermediate/personal-agents","track":"everyone","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Personal Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":", holds who an agent is. And "},{"type":"code","text":"DESIGN.md"},{"type":"text","text":" holds how its work should look."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-they-still-do-not-do","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What They Still Do Not Do"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"These tools give you a good-looking screen fast. They do not give you what a designer gives you, which is knowing what the screen is "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"for"}]},{"type":"text","text":": what the user is trying to finish, and what should be easy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","text":" is the clearest gap. It means everyone can use the screen, including a person who uses only a keyboard or a screen reader (software that reads the screen aloud). Generated screens often get it wrong:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"text whose contrast fails the measured ratio, which you can only measure, not see"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"no visible marker of where the keyboard is on the page"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a control that only works with a mouse"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"an icon button with no name, so a screen reader cannot announce it"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"None of these show up in a screenshot. Someone has to check them."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"words-you-now-know","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Words You Now Know"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Design system"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the design decisions a brand makes once (colours, fonts, spacing, buttons)."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"DESIGN.md"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a file that gives those decisions to the AI."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Google Stitch / Claude Design"}]},{"type":"text","text":": tools that turn a description into a working design."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","text":": making sure everyone can use a screen, including with a keyboard or screen reader."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"summary","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"AI can design a screen from a description, but without your brand rules every screen comes out different. Put the rules in "},{"type":"code","text":"DESIGN.md"},{"type":"text","text":", with both the exact values and the intent, and every screen matches. And check what a screenshot cannot show: whether everyone can actually use it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Quick check"}]},{"type":"text","text":": Why do two AI-made screens look like they came from different companies? And name one accessibility problem a screenshot will not show you."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-articles","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"📄 Articles"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://developers.googleblog.com/stitch-a-new-way-to-design-uis/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"From idea to app: Introducing Stitch"}]},{"type":"text","text":": Google's announcement, and where DESIGN.md comes from"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"️-tools","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🛠️ Tools"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://stitch.withgoogle.com/"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Google Stitch"}]},{"type":"text","text":": turns a description or a sketch into a real design"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://claude.ai/design"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Claude Design"}]},{"type":"text","text":": makes prototypes as real code"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"️-repositories","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"🗃️ Repositories"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://github.com/VoltAgent/awesome-design-md"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"awesome-design-md"}]},{"type":"text","text":": ready-made DESIGN.md files for well-known brands"}]}]}]}],"draft":null,"quiz":[{"question":"What do Google Stitch and Claude Design have in common?","options":["They need a finished design in Figma before they can start.","They check each screen for contrast and focus problems.","They turn a description into a screen and output real code.","They keep every screen consistent without any extra file."],"answer":2,"explain":"Both tools turn a description into a screen and produce real HTML and CSS, not only a picture. Stitch can also export to Figma, and Claude Design can read the design system in a codebase."},{"question":"An agent writes a different interface every time. What is the cause?","options":["It must invent every design decision each time, because it cannot know yours.","It reads an old DESIGN.md file that no longer matches the product.","It cannot write real HTML and CSS, so it draws a new picture each time.","It follows the palette in the prompt too strictly on every screen."],"answer":0,"explain":"Without your decisions in a file, the agent invents plausible ones, and they change each time. A palette in the prompt works for one screen, but not for the second."},{"question":"DESIGN.md holds two kinds of content. What are they?","options":["Screenshots of each screen, and the HTML that produced them.","The rules for the repository, and a description of who the agent is.","A list of brand names, and a link to the website of each brand.","Tokens with exact values, and the intent of the design in words."],"answer":3,"explain":"The tokens are exact values, such as hex codes, the type scale and spacing steps. The intent says what the design tries to feel like and what it avoids, which a token list cannot carry."},{"question":"The module compares DESIGN.md with AGENTS.md and SOUL.md. What do the three files share?","options":["They each hold the colour palette and the spacing steps of the product.","They are markdown files in the repository, for humans and models to read.","They are each produced by Google Stitch from a prompt or a sketch.","They are written for human designers only, and agents do not read them."],"answer":1,"explain":"A human can write markdown comfortably, and a model can parse it reliably. DESIGN.md goes at the repository root with that exact name, so most agents find it."},{"question":"A generated interface looks right in a screenshot. What does the module tell you to check yourself?","options":["The Figma export, because the layout often breaks when it leaves Stitch.","The HTML, because these tools often return a picture instead of code.","Accessibility: contrast, focus states, keyboard access and icon button names.","The token names, because agents cannot read hex codes from DESIGN.md."],"answer":2,"explain":"Generated interfaces often fail on contrast, focus and keyboard access, and a screenshot does not show these problems. The tools also do not know what the screen is for."}]}